+# Feature 001 — Viewport Motion Blur
+
+## Using the feature
+
+In Cycles Render Properties, expand **Motion Blur**. **Viewport Motion Blur** is
+directly under the existing master toggle, before Position. The new checkbox is
+off by default and requires master Motion Blur. Enable both and enter Rendered
+viewport shading while paused on an animated frame.
+
+This uses the existing true Cycles shutter-time motion system. It is not vector
+blur, a screen-space filter, or previous-frame smearing. No separate viewport
+motion sample controls are introduced.
+
+## Existing controls
+
+- **Shutter, Position and curve:** reuse Cycles shutter configuration. Rolling
+  shutter uses the existing camera/kernel path.
+- **Object Use Motion Blur:** disables motion sampling for that object as in the
+  existing Cycles object controls.
+- **Motion Steps:** uses each object's existing motion-step count. Higher settings
+  sample curved rotation more accurately; a rotating blade is a useful comparison.
+  Changing one object's settings must not corrupt other objects' motion data.
+- **Deformation Motion:** reuses existing deformation samples where supported.
+  Tests cover shape-key deformation and stable-topology Geometry Nodes deformation.
+- **Camera motion:** camera view samples animated cameras alongside animated objects.
+  Free-perspective navigation itself does not represent a shutter-time camera animation.
+- **Frame/subframe edits:** sample evaluation restores original and evaluated time,
+  including negative fractional frames, without moving the user's timeline through
+  shutter samples.
+- **Accumulation:** a paused viewport should keep accumulating normally after edits
+  settle. The tests check actual render batches and resets, not just identical images.
+- **GPU Compute and denoising:** existing device and denoising paths remain in place.
+  Automated evidence is CPU-based and includes OpenImageDenoise; it does not prove
+  every GPU backend or driver configuration.
+- **F12:** the viewport checkbox does not select final-render motion blur. Existing
+  master and object controls still apply. The regression compares checkbox off/on
+  pairs and retains the original F12 image baseline.
+
+## Example scene
+
+Open [motion-test.blend](../../../tests/viewport-motion-blur/scenes/motion-test.blend).
+It contains an animated cube and camera at frame 50. Enable Rendered shading,
+toggle Viewport Motion Blur, vary shutter 0.5/1.0 and Position, and compare F12 with
+the viewport checkbox off/on. Use a separate save location if editing this fixture.
+
+## Limits and manual checks
+
+Correct paused-frame rendering is the priority; real-time playback is not a target.
+The automated suite uses small CPU scenes. Arbitrary simulations/caches, changing
+topology, hair/particles, advanced camera projections, multi-window/undo behavior,
+and long sessions are not exhaustively validated. Viewport/final visibility and
+modifier differences still apply. Custom shutter curves are reused but not separately
+exercised by the current suite.
+
+Handler-driven animation or drivers explicitly reading the original timeline can
+differ from F12: shutter sampling deliberately does not move that timeline or invoke
+frame handlers. Inactive depsgraph evaluation does not establish support for arbitrary
+uncached simulations.
+
+Manually stress the production GPU scene in free perspective and camera view, with
+denoising off/on. Let samples accumulate, orbit and stop, change frame/subframe and
+shutter, edit Motion Steps repeatedly on several objects, and toggle deformation.
+Compare representative F12 renders with the viewport checkbox off/on.
+
+[Implementation map](implementation.md) · [Regression instructions](testing.md) ·
+[Historical evidence](provenance.md)
